<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mạc Thiên Tứ</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1718">1718</a>–<a href="/years/1780">1780</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Mac Thien Tu, Mo Tianci</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mạc Thiên Tứ was the second ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-tien">Hà Tiên</a> principality on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Thailand</a>, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-cuu">Mạc Cửu</a> in 1735 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyễn lords</a>. A patron of letters, he gathered Chinese and Vietnamese poets in a literary society and intervened in Cambodian successions. Driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a> of Siam in 1771, he later went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, where he died in 1780.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mac-thien-tu.jpg" alt="Mạc Thiên Tứ" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bùi Thụy Đào Nguyên · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%E1%BB%99_M%E1%BA%A1c_Thi%C3%AAn_T%E1%BB%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mạc Thiên Tứ, also known as Mạc Thiên Tích or Mạc Tông, was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-cuu">Mạc Cửu</a> and a Vietnamese woman from Biên Hòa. The Veritable Records of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> call him Mo Shilin. After his father's death the Nguyễn lord gave him the title of Tổng binh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-tien">Hà Tiên</a> and the noble title Marquess Tông Đức. In a letter of 1742 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a>, he styled himself king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His rule is remembered as the golden age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-tien">Hà Tiên</a>. He set up a regular bureaucracy, enlarged the army, and built castles and business streets, and the town became the trade centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-delta">Mekong Delta</a> and the best-known port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Siam</a>. He built many schools, treated all religions evenhandedly, and allowed the French priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-pigneau-de-behaine">Pierre Pigneau de Behaine</a> to preach. Vietnamese sources record that in 1739 he defeated a Cambodian invasion, after which Hà Tiên was free of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>.</p><p>In Cambodian affairs he adopted the prince Ang Ton, escorted him home and crowned him king as Outey II, receiving in return Hương Úc, Cần Bột, Trực Sâm, Sài Mạt and Linh Quỳnh. After the second fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> he sheltered the Siamese prince Chao Chui, refused to hand him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a>, sent a fleet against Chantaburi in 1769 and told Qing officials that Taksin was a usurper. In 1771 Taksin took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-tien">Hà Tiên</a> and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-dinh">Gia Định</a>, returning two years later with the Nguyễn lord's help.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> defeated Nguyễn Phúc Thuần, who was executed in 1777, Tứ refused to surrender to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quang-trung">Nguyễn Huệ</a> and fled by way of Phú Quốc to Siam, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a> received him and gave him the title Phraya Rachasethi Yuan. In 1780 Taksin imprisoned him, according to Vietnamese records because of a letter forged by Nguyễn Huệ, according to Thai records because he planned to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-tien">Hà Tiên</a>. He killed himself in prison, and two of his sons and his wife were executed. Three other sons were hidden and later lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, and in 1789 his grandson Mạc Công Bính brought his body back to Hà Tiên for burial.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mạc Thiên Tứ” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Mac Thien Tu</b> it became a cultural centre where Chinese and Vietnamese poets gathered, and its rulers intervened in Cambodian successions.</blockquote><a href="/state/ha-tien-principality">Ha Tien Principality</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Krong Kep, in what is now <a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. Died in Thonburi Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=10.48,104.32&amp;year=1758" aria-label="Krong Kep on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7425 1772 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mạc Thiên Tứ"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7875 2042h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7875 2042h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=10.48,104.32&amp;year=1758">What was here in 1758? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
